WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.249 --> 00:00:01.249
The Mummy

00:00:01.249 --> 00:00:05.288
When you've got as much clout as Tom Cruise,
you can make all sorts of stipulations when

00:00:05.288 --> 00:00:06.728
you sign on for a movie.

00:00:06.728 --> 00:00:12.399
Reportedly, Cruise's demands for the 2017
Mummy movie included near-total creative control.

00:00:12.399 --> 00:00:17.059
As later outlined in a Variety investigation,
Cruise was accused of micromanaging almost

00:00:17.059 --> 00:00:21.359
every aspect of the film's production, changing
everything from the script to the way it was

00:00:21.359 --> 00:00:22.359
marketed.

00:00:22.359 --> 00:00:26.379
Perhaps most notably, Cruise allegedly insisted
that his character, Nick Morton, be given

00:00:26.379 --> 00:00:28.189
more screen time than the mummy.

00:00:28.189 --> 00:00:32.599
His presence was beefed up accordingly, effectively
turning the horror franchise reboot into another

00:00:32.599 --> 00:00:35.949
of the star's blockbuster action vehicles.

00:00:35.949 --> 00:00:37.059
Snakes on a Plane

00:00:37.059 --> 00:00:41.139
Just like snakes have all sorts of reasons
for getting on planes, stars have all sorts

00:00:41.139 --> 00:00:43.569
of reasons for agreeing to take on a role.

00:00:43.569 --> 00:00:47.699
Samuel L. Jackson, for example, admitted that
he only wanted to star in Snakes on a Plane

00:00:47.699 --> 00:00:48.889
because of its title.

00:00:48.889 --> 00:00:54.049
When executives considered changing the title
to Pacific Flight 121, he personally intervened,

00:00:54.049 --> 00:00:56.739
telling them it was "the stupidest damn thing
I ever heard."

00:00:56.739 --> 00:01:01.569
Jackson also advocated for more violence and
profanity, an issue because the studio envisioned

00:01:01.569 --> 00:01:04.319
Snakes as a campy PG-13 action movie.

00:01:04.319 --> 00:01:09.179
Eventually, they agreed with his more adult-oriented
take and he agreed to re-shoots to secure

00:01:09.179 --> 00:01:13.539
an R rating… which just served to make the
edited TV version all the more ridiculous.

00:01:13.539 --> 00:01:19.849
"I have had it with these monkey-fighting
snakes on this Monday to Friday plane."

00:01:19.849 --> 00:01:21.159
The Avengers

00:01:21.159 --> 00:01:25.329
In the original script for The Avengers, the
scene immediately following the near-defeat

00:01:25.329 --> 00:01:28.259
of the Chitauri was much less light-hearted.

00:01:28.259 --> 00:01:31.669
After sending the bomb through the wormhole
and falling back to Earth, Tony Stark simply

00:01:31.669 --> 00:01:34.609
asked "What's next?" after being roused by
his comrades.

00:01:34.609 --> 00:01:39.539
Robert Downey Jr. felt this line fell a little
flat and suggested trying something different.

00:01:39.539 --> 00:01:43.289
According to Entertainment Weekly, director
Joss Whedon put together three pages of new

00:01:43.289 --> 00:01:45.489
lines based on Downey's suggestion.

00:01:45.489 --> 00:01:49.199
In the newly revamped scene, Iron Man has
a little more banter with his teammates.

00:01:49.199 --> 00:01:51.229
"Have you ever tried shawarma?"

00:01:51.229 --> 00:01:53.699
"There's a shawarma joint three blocks from
here."

00:01:53.699 --> 00:01:55.869
"I don't know what it is but I want to try
it."

00:01:55.869 --> 00:01:59.599
Everyone liked the line so much that Whedon
filmed a bonus post-credits scene of the entire

00:01:59.599 --> 00:02:00.869
cast eating.

00:02:00.869 --> 00:02:04.018
But since production had already wrapped,
he had to film the scene when the cast came

00:02:04.018 --> 00:02:05.839
back together for the premiere.

00:02:05.839 --> 00:02:09.929
According to EW, Chris Evans even had to wear
a fake jaw prosthetic to hide the beard he'd

00:02:09.929 --> 00:02:10.929
grown.

00:02:10.929 --> 00:02:13.269
That's why Captain America isn't eating with
the rest of the team.

00:02:13.269 --> 00:02:17.929
And it was all because Robert Downey, Jr.
wanted to spice up his lines.

00:02:17.929 --> 00:02:19.459
Jurassic World

00:02:19.459 --> 00:02:23.779
Critics were pretty happy with Jurassic World,
but that didn't stop audiences from complaining

00:02:23.779 --> 00:02:29.219
that Bryce Dallas Howard's character somehow
outruns 22 tons of T-rex in high heels.

00:02:29.219 --> 00:02:33.409
Director Colin Trevorrow was completely aware
of how ridiculous this was, and reportedly

00:02:33.409 --> 00:02:37.539
spent much of the film's production trying
to convince her to literally slip into something

00:02:37.539 --> 00:02:38.719
more comfortable.

00:02:38.719 --> 00:02:42.688
Howard refused, insisting that her character
had to wear heels the whole time.

00:02:42.688 --> 00:02:44.059
"We'll find them."

00:02:44.059 --> 00:02:46.519
"You'll last two minutes in there!

00:02:46.519 --> 00:02:49.199
Less in those ridiculous shoes."

00:02:49.199 --> 00:02:53.239
Trevorrow would later admit that he wasn't
sure exactly why Howard was so insistent,

00:02:53.239 --> 00:02:55.999
but he respected her decision, and the heels
stayed in.

00:02:55.999 --> 00:03:00.469
Howard herself would later say she never expected
wearing the heels to be such a major talking

00:03:00.469 --> 00:03:04.499
point, but she was happy to have the effort
she put into running through the jungle acknowledged,

00:03:09.263 --> 00:03:10.843
Clash of the Titans

00:03:10.843 --> 00:03:14.602
Bubo the clockwork owl is one of the most
memorable parts of the original Clash of the

00:03:14.602 --> 00:03:20.033
Titans, so naturally, director Louis Leterrier
planned on making it a major part of his 2010

00:03:20.033 --> 00:03:21.033
remake.

00:03:21.033 --> 00:03:23.082
Unfortunately, Sam Worthington hated Bubo.

00:03:23.082 --> 00:03:27.053
Leterrier later recounted that Worthington
complained about the owl at every opportunity,

00:03:27.053 --> 00:03:31.933
going as far to accuse the director of trying
to ruin his career by making him star opposite

00:03:31.933 --> 00:03:33.283
something so ridiculous.

00:03:33.283 --> 00:03:37.653
Leterrier goaded Worthington by saying he
wasn't trying to ruin his career, just damage

00:03:37.653 --> 00:03:40.003
it, which didn't exactly help matters.

00:03:40.003 --> 00:03:44.253
Ultimately, to appease his star, Leterrier
took the owl out of the film, relegating it

00:03:44.253 --> 00:03:46.683
to a brief cameo.

00:03:46.683 --> 00:03:47.683
Shrek

00:03:47.683 --> 00:03:51.893
Shrek's signature Scottish accent went through
a couple of major changes before audiences

00:03:51.893 --> 00:03:53.933
fell in love with the big green ogre.

00:03:53.933 --> 00:03:58.063
Saturday Night Live vet Chris Farley was originally
supposed to voice the character, but passed

00:03:58.063 --> 00:04:02.333
away before he could finish recording his
lines; after his passing, Mike Myers stepped

00:04:02.333 --> 00:04:03.333
in.

00:04:03.333 --> 00:04:06.683
It wasn't until after roughly a third of the
movie had been animated, however, that Myers

00:04:06.683 --> 00:04:08.973
decided Shrek should be Scottish.

00:04:08.973 --> 00:04:13.373
Myers' reasoning was that since the movie's
villain, Lord Farquaad, spoke with an upper-class

00:04:13.373 --> 00:04:18.103
English accent, Shrek should sound more blue-collar,
to highlight the difference between them.

00:04:18.103 --> 00:04:19.512
"It's hideous!"

00:04:19.512 --> 00:04:23.033
"Aw, that's not very nice.

00:04:23.033 --> 00:04:24.973
It's just a donkey."

00:04:24.973 --> 00:04:29.533
He also felt that the Scottish accent lent
itself better to dramatic, abrupt shifts in

00:04:29.533 --> 00:04:32.973
tone and would allow him to emote in a more
exaggerated fashion.

00:04:32.973 --> 00:04:37.292
DreamWorks exec Jeffrey Katzenberg pegged
the cost of reworking the already animated

00:04:37.292 --> 00:04:42.762
scenes at 4 to 5 million dollars—roughly
10 percent of the movie's overall budget.

00:04:42.762 --> 00:04:44.702
Star Wars: Attack of the Clones

00:04:44.702 --> 00:04:49.363
After watching a rough cut of the Battle for
Geonosis with Star Wars creator George Lucas,

00:04:49.363 --> 00:04:53.172
Samuel L. Jackson noticed that it was hard
to spot his own character, Mace Windu, amongst

00:04:53.172 --> 00:04:55.073
the dozens of Jedi on screen.

00:04:55.073 --> 00:04:59.813
He asked Lucas if it'd be possible for Windu
to wield a purple lightsaber—partly because

00:04:59.813 --> 00:05:03.213
it'd stand out more, and partly because purple
is Jackson's favorite color.

00:05:03.213 --> 00:05:06.133
"So I said to George, 'You think I can get
a purple lightsaber?'

00:05:06.133 --> 00:05:09.463
'Lightsabers are green or lightsabers are
red.'

00:05:09.463 --> 00:05:11.232
'Yeah but I want a purple one.'"

00:05:11.232 --> 00:05:15.913
Lucas initially turned down the request, but
later decided to let Jackson get his purple

00:05:15.913 --> 00:05:16.913
lightsaber.

00:05:16.913 --> 00:05:19.553
Because when Samuel L. Jackson asks for something,
he gets it.

00:05:19.553 --> 00:05:24.433
"I have the real one at home that says 'Bad
Motherf---er' right here."

00:05:24.433 --> 00:05:26.173
"Are you serious?"

00:05:26.173 --> 00:05:27.173
"Yeah!"

00:05:27.303 --> 00:05:28.993
A Million Ways to Die in the West

00:05:28.993 --> 00:05:33.053
Family Guy has mocked a lot of people over
the years, but one joke in particular came

00:05:33.053 --> 00:05:35.172
back to haunt creator Seth MacFarlane.

00:05:35.172 --> 00:05:38.773
Family Guy made a joke about how Liam Neeson
would never be able to play a cowboy in a

00:05:38.773 --> 00:05:43.383
western because of his inability to convincingly
mask his distinctive Irish accent.

00:05:43.383 --> 00:05:45.613
"This glen's gonna be tough to traverse.

00:05:45.613 --> 00:05:48.933
And the river's gotta be 50, 60 meters wide.

00:05:48.933 --> 00:05:50.693
And God knows how many fathoms."

00:05:50.693 --> 00:05:54.713
Years later, when MacFarlane asked Liam Neeson
to star in his comedy western A Million Ways

00:05:54.713 --> 00:05:58.093
to Die in the West, Neeson said he'd do it,
with one condition.

00:05:58.093 --> 00:06:03.404
"When Seth called me up to ask that I do this
film, I said I'd do it on one condition, that

00:06:03.404 --> 00:06:06.093
I can use my own Irish accent."

00:06:06.093 --> 00:06:10.523
MacFarlane agreed, and Neeson's character
became an inexplicably Irish cowboy—all

00:06:10.523 --> 00:06:15.023
because of a throwaway line in an old episode
of Family Guy suggesting that it'd be pretty

00:06:15.023 --> 00:06:18.273
stupid for anyone to hire Liam Neeson to star
in a western.

00:06:19.653 --> 00:06:22.473
"You really do have a death wish, don't you."

00:06:22.473 --> 00:06:23.513
Thanks for watching!

00:06:23.513 --> 00:06:25.993
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:25.993 --> 00:06:28.062
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

